hello and welcome to the chichi science  show where in this video we're going to  revisit  resveratrol and look at the latest data  showing the results of some recent  human trials whereby patients with  either heart failure or muscular  dystrophy  were treated with resveratrol now the  last time i spoke about resveratrol i  feel like i may have caused a little bit  of the stress  mainly because we looked at the  publication that suggested that  resveratrol could be inducing  replicative stress in cells  due to the effect that part of  resveratrol action seems to be by  inhibiting dna synthesis  but as i also mentioned near the end of  that video it seems to be that  considering the dose of resveratrol is  very important  for delineating its beneficial and  detrimental impacts for the body so  we'll be taking an extra close attention  to the doses used  in these two latest publications showing  some human data  whereby patients were taking resveratrol  to either alleviate symptoms of muscular  dystrophy  or heart disease so just quickly then  what actually is resveratrol  well resveratrol is a naturally  occurring phenylpropanoid that's found  in a variety of different foods  for example the skin of grapes  blueberries raspberries and also in  peanuts  and resveratrol is a phytoelectron which  means that it's produced by plants when  under stressful conditions  such as being infected by pathogens or  being physically harmed  so what does resveratrol actually do  well there's a lot of hype surrounding  resveratrol as it's been shown that  it can extend lifespan in a variety of  different model organisms  such as yeast worms fruit flies as well  as mice when fed on a high fat diet  however to get the same level of  resveratrol as performed in these  studies it's been calculated that you  would need to drink around 111  glasses of red wine so instead people  have looked to supplementation  but what is the actual mechanism behind  this apparent increase in lifespan  seen in the different model organisms  well it's thought that resveratrol acts  by  increasing the activity of the protein  set one  cert one comes from a family of proteins  known as sertoins that are  nad plus dependent enzymes that play  multiple roles within the cell  one of which seems to be activation of  stress resistance pathways  however the biochemical underpinning of  resveratrol isn't fully understood yet  nonetheless due to the connections  between resveratrol and  activation or stress resistance pathways  respiratory was being investigated to  see if it could be effective for  ameliorating  for a variety of different diseases so  this nicely brings us on to these two  recent papers  that give the results of some human  trials but by their uterus ferrotroll to  ameliorate the symptoms of either heart  failure  or muscular dystrophy so let's just  begin with  this first study resveratrol improves  heart function  by moderating inflammatory processes in  patients with systolic heart failure  now the prevalence of heart failure is  continuing to rise throughout the world  however previous work has shown that  resveratrol supplementation  in rodent models prevented the  development of heart failure  when their heart muscles had been  damaged however until this publication  the impact of resveratrol on human  patients with heart failure had yet to  be assessed  so in this study they performed a  double-blind randomized  placebo-controlled study  whereby 30 patients were assigned  resveratrol and 30 patients were given a  placebo  and the dose of resveratrol that the  patients were given were  two 50 mg capsules so they were taking  100 milligrams of resveratrol per day  and they did this for three months  and just to clarify all of the patients  had heart failure with reduced ejection  fraction  and so a variety of different  measurements were taken before and after  the three months of treatment  one of the significant differences was a  reduction in the level of two  inflammatory cytokines  interleukin-1 and interleukin-6 moreover  there was a reduction in galectin-3  which is often used as a biomarker for  heart failure and more strikingly they  saw that  the left ventricular ejection fraction  also significantly increased in the  group given resveratrol  suggesting that there seems to be  improvements in their heart performance  as this also correlated with an increase  in the six minute walk distance in the  respiratory group  so the results from this study seem  pretty promising for the use of  resveratrol  in systolic heart failure patients as an  addition to the standard therapy  however as the authors also note there  are a few limitations  firstly the low number of enrolled  patients being only 30 given resveratrol  and also the relatively short follow-up  periods so if these two things can be  improved  it would be much more convincing to  prove the efficacy of resveratrol  supplementation  in systolic heart failure as well as to  maybe try and better understand the  exact biochemical and cellular mechanism  by looking at more biomarkers so let's  now take a look at the second  publication  resveratrol improves motor function in  patients with muscular dystrophies  so as stated in the title this is a  phase 2a study  and these are typically very small  clinical trials  and that's pretty evident in this study  where they only had 11 patients  and the patients had either dot chen  becca or fukuyama muscular dystrophy  and in comparison to the heart failure  study where they were taking  100 milligrams per day here they  initially started taking 500 milligrams  a day  which was then increased every eight  weeks to a thousand milligrams  to 1500 milligrams or 1.5 grams per day  so the key differences so far well is  firstly obviously the different diseases  but secondly there is a much lower  number of patients in the muscular  dystrophy trial  however they were using a higher dose  and they also did it for a longer time  period  here doing it for 24 weeks which is  around six months in comparison to the  three months study in the heart failure  trial  and so muscular dystrophies are  inherited disorders that are  characterized by  progressive muscle weakness and  disability so in this study they were  looking  primarily to see what impact resveratrol  had  on motor function and they did this by  looking at the motor function measure  scale  muscular strength monitored by muscle  testing and also by looking at the serum  creatine kinase levels  and interestingly as a secondary outcome  they also wanted to look at the  tolerability  of resveratrol and if there are any  adverse effects  and one of the reasons they decided to  use resveratrol is  a previous study found that cert 1  overexpression  so cert 1 being a protein that  resveratrol was thought to activate  in the skeletal muscles of mdx mice  which are an animal model of doctrine  muscular dystrophy  decrease the serum creatine kinase  levels tissue fibrosis and myofibril  damage  and increases their ability to run long  distances compared to the control mice  so what about in humans then well they  found that the mean  motor function measure score increased  significantly after 24 weeks of  medication  and they also found a twofold increase  in the mean  quantitative muscle testing scores and  also they found the mean  creating kinase levels decreased  considerably by around 34  now i've emphasized the word mean  because there is quite  a bit of variation between the different  patients  however more interestingly in their  study they found that diarrhea and  abdominal pain was noted in six and  three patients respectively  and this resulted in some of them  reducing their dose from  1.5 grams per day down to 1 gram per day  and so i think the study highlights  again the importance of really trying to  understand the impact of dose  on the impact that it has on cells which  was more of the point i was trying to  make in my previous video  and so obviously from a study like this  where it was only done in 11 patients  it is still really hard to take any  significance from it  nonetheless this does show great  potential for using resveratrol for  ameliorating the symptoms of muscular  dystrophy  and i guess trying to bring the two  trials together this is kind of the  the main point of these early stage  trials is just to see  the primary outcome in terms of  ameliorating  impacts of the disease by the treatment  and to see if there's any consequences  or any side effects to taking the drugs  and so i think definitely  dose is really important when it comes  to resveratrol  and it would be nicer now to see these  clinical trials  expand and do it for longer time periods  to really see  the long-term impact on resveratrol  supplementation so hopefully this video  has given you a good overview if not  brief  to the different trials i've linked down  the papers in the description  and so i hope you found something and as  always thanks for listening
 

